# .gitignore
#
# THE RULE. Every path in this repository is exactly one of three things, and
# knowing which one it is decides whether it belongs in git. When you add
# something, decide its bucket first and put the entry in that section.
#
#   1. COMMITTED CONFIG - non-secret settings that make the lab portable.
#      Ports, hostnames, image tags and digests, provider version pins.
#      Clone this elsewhere and it stands up the same. Not listed here.
#
#   2. MACHINE-LOCAL STATE - real, but not portable. Container data, rendered
#      files, anything holding an address discovered on this host. Deleting it
#      costs a re-run, nothing more.
#
#   3. SECRETS - never, on any branch. If one lands in history, ROTATING THE
#      SECRET IS THE FIX; deleting the file is not.
#
# Two traps worth knowing before you edit this file.
#
#   A pattern containing a slash is relative to THIS file's directory. So
#   `docker/vault/certs/` stops matching the moment that tree moves. Where a
#   rule can be written without a slash it is, because a bare `certs/` matches
#   at any depth and survives a reorganisation. `reference/` keeps its own
#   .gitignore for the same reason.
#
#   A rule that matches nothing looks exactly like no rule. `git check-ignore`
#   answers for a path whether or not it exists, so assert on the paths you
#   care about rather than reading this file and believing it. Phase 7.2 wires
#   those assertions into `make lint`.
#
# Why this file exists before anything generates a secret: the first run that
# writes a private key must not also be the run that decides whether it is
# ignored.


# --- 1. Committed config ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Nothing is ignored here, but two things are easy to ignore by mistake:
#
#   .terraform.lock.hcl   COMMIT IT. It pins provider versions and is what
#                         makes an init reproducible on another machine.
#   *.tmpl                COMMIT the templates. Their rendered output is
#                         bucket 2 and is ignored below.


# --- 2. Machine-local state -------------------------------------------------

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~

# Our own run state: tracker, generated inventory, scratch.
/state/

# Container runtime state, at any depth.
data/
logs/

# Rendered from a committed .tmpl. The template is the source of truth.
.env
*.rendered
docker/coredns/zones/*.zone
docker/proxy/conf/*.conf

# CloudStack's vendored installer writes these beside itself.
cloudstack-installer-tracker.conf
cloudstack-installer.log
installer.log

# Gitea runner registration. Single-use token exchanged for this file.
.runner

# Terraform working state. The lock file is bucket 1 - see above.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
tfplan
plan.json
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Packer output. A qcow2 is 1-2 GB and is rebuilt from the template.
packer/output-*/
packer/packer_cache/
*.qcow2
*.img

# Ansible generated inventory and retry files.
ansible/inventory/generated*
*.retry

# MinIO client config - holds endpoint aliases AND their credentials.
.mc/


# --- 3. Secrets -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Anything matching here is a credential. There is no branch on which one of
# these may be committed.

secrets/

# TLS material, at any depth. Ignoring the directory rather than the extension
# means the key and its certificate go together and nothing depends on
# guessing an extension correctly.
certs/
*.pem
*.key
*.key.enc
*.crt
*.csr

# Vault's two tier-1 credentials - the only two that cannot live in Vault,
# because they are what opens it. Kept in separate files so that rotating the
# token never rewrites the file holding the irreplaceable unseal key.
vault-init.json*
unseal-key*
root-token*

# Vault AppRole. Both halves are credentials.
*role-id*
*secret-id*

# SSH keys.
id_rsa
id_ed25519
*_rsa
*_ed25519

# WireGuard private keys. Generated on the VM and never moved (see the
# wireguard role) - so one appearing here means something went wrong.
*.wgkey
privatekey*

# Terraform variable files. Credentials arrive as TF_VAR_* from Vault at run
# time; a .tfvars file on disk is how that discipline gets broken.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example
